  localStorage is the single source of truth: every read hits storage and validates the expiry stamp, so there's no
  cached copy to go stale. Cross-tab consistency is free — a second tab reads the same storage. Consumers pull the ID
  when they need it. Tradeoff: no way to [3mreact[0m to login/logout, so anything that needs to update on a login in another
  tab has to re-read on its own schedule. Complexity: trivial.

  [1mB. Observable session store[0m — same storage, but the module keeps an in-memory copy hydrated at startup, writes through
  to localStorage, and exposes [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m plus a [38;5;153mwindow[39m [38;5;153mstorage[39m-event listener so other tabs propagate. Consumers
  react to identity changes instead of polling. Buys you live UI updates across tabs; costs you a cache that can diverge
  from storage and a subscription lifecycle to get right. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mC. Pure session rules + injected storage adapter[0m — the session record and its rules (parse, validate, expire) are pure
  functions with no browser dependency; a thin adapter binds them to localStorage at the app edge. Fully testable in
  Node with no DOM harness, which matters here because the repo has [3mno[0m test setup at all, and it makes the eventual
  "what if the server also returns a token" question a swap at one boundary. Costs the most files and indirection for an
  app this small. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It meets every requirement you named with the least machinery, and critically it doesn't foreclose the
  others: B's [38;5;153msubscribe[39m can be added later without changing A's function signatures, and C's pure-rules split can be
  extracted from A's internals when there's a test runner to justify it. Starting at B or C means buying cross-tab
  reactivity and an injection seam before any consumer exists that needs them — and you said the other forms are [3mlater[0m,
  not now.
